\begin{frontmatter}

\title{Janus-Face-Selective Li$^+$ Binding and Anisotropic Ion Transport in PEO/LiTFSI Composite Polymer Electrolytes Reinforced by WSSe Monolayer Fillers: An Atomistic Molecular Dynamics Study}

\author[]{Ashwani Kushwaha}
\ead{ashwani12@iitb.ac.in}
% \author[bp]{Gy\"orgy B\'anhegyi\corref{cor1}}
% \ead{gyorgy.banhegyi56@gmail.com}
% \cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author}
\address{Department of Mechanical Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology Bombay, Mumbai 400076, India}
% \address[bp]{Independent Consultant, Budapest, Hungary}

\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Two-dimensional fillers have emerged as a promising route to enhance ion transport and mechanical stability in polymer-based composite polymer electrolytes (CPEs) for solid-state lithium batteries. Janus transition-metal dichalcogenides such as \WSSe, whose two faces present distinct chemical environments (S and Se), introduce a built-in out-of-plane polarity that may further bias Li$^+$ distribution and transport at the filler--polymer interface. Here we report an all-atom molecular dynamics study of a single Janus \WSSe monolayer embedded in a poly(ethylene oxide)/lithium bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonyl)imide (PEO/\LiTFSI) matrix (ether-oxygen-to-Li ratio EO:Li $=$ 16:1) at $400$~K, combining the modified Stillinger--Weber potential for \WSSe with the all-atom OPLS-AA force field (Li$^+$ charge scaled to $0.75$) for the polymer electrolyte. Over a $30$~ns production trajectory we extract: (i) Li$^+$ self-diffusion split into in-plane and out-of-plane components, (ii) the radial distribution function and coordination number of Li$^+$ with ether oxygens, (iii) the z-resolved Li$^+$ density profile around the sheet, and (iv) face-resolved Li$^+$ contact statistics. Li$^+$ exhibits the canonical $\sim6$-fold ether-oxygen coordination at $\sim2.13$~\AA, in close agreement with prior experimental and computational reports, validating the force-field setup. Li$^+$ transport is strongly anisotropic, with $\Dxy/\Dz = 2.4$ ($\Dxy=4.4\times10^{-7}$ and $\Dz=1.8\times10^{-7}$~cm$^2$/s), establishing the impermeable WSSe layer as a directional ion-transport feature. Most strikingly, Li$^+$ accumulation within $3$~\AA{} of the Se-face exceeds that of the S-face by a factor of $\sim3.5$ (significance $6.2\sigma$), demonstrating that the Janus chemistry is imprinted on the local cation distribution even within a solvating polymer matrix. These findings establish atomistic design rules for asymmetric 2D fillers in composite polymer electrolytes and motivate experimental synthesis of Janus-TMD-reinforced CPEs for directional Li$^+$ transport.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
Composite polymer electrolyte \sep Janus 2D materials \sep WSSe monolayer \sep PEO/LiTFSI \sep molecular dynamics \sep Li$^+$ transport anisotropy \sep solid-state batteries
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

%% ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

The push toward solid-state lithium batteries (SSLBs) has revived interest in polymer-based composite polymer electrolytes (CPEs) as a route to high energy density combined with mechanical resilience against lithium dendrite penetration \cite{nano_micro_letters_cpe_review}. Among polymer matrices, poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO) doped with lithium bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonyl)imide (\LiTFSI) is one of the most thoroughly characterised systems, owing to its strong Li$^+$ solvation by ether-oxygen lone pairs and the highly delocalised, weakly coordinating nature of the TFSI$^-$ anion \cite{he_jpcb_2020_polarity, marchiori_electrochim_2017}. Yet neat PEO/\LiTFSI exhibits two persistent limitations: a room-temperature ionic conductivity that is one to two orders of magnitude below liquid electrolytes, and a relatively low elastic modulus that limits its ability to suppress dendrite growth.

\paragraph{Filler strategies in CPEs.} To overcome these limitations, a wide range of inorganic fillers have been incorporated into the PEO/salt matrix \cite{nano_micro_letters_cpe_review}. Early work focused on zero-dimensional oxide nanoparticles (e.g., SiO$_2$, TiO$_2$, Al$_2$O$_3$), which were shown by Croce et al. to enhance ionic conductivity by up to three orders of magnitude through suppression of PEO crystallinity. More recently, attention has shifted toward two-dimensional fillers --- including graphene oxide, MXenes (Ti$_3$C$_2$T$_x$), black phosphorus, and transition-metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) \cite{pan_nanoscale_adv_2019_mxene, rojaee_2d_bp}. The 2D geometry provides three key benefits over 0D analogues at equal loading: (i) a much larger polymer--filler interfacial area per unit mass, (ii) a long-range orientational template that can bias polymer chain alignment, and (iii) the ability to render the composite's transport properties anisotropic --- a feature that has been noted phenomenologically but rarely quantified at the atomistic level \cite{pan_nanoscale_adv_2019_mxene}.

\paragraph{Janus 2D materials.} A more recent development is the experimental realisation of \textit{Janus} TMDs such as \WSSe, MoSSe, and MoSTe, where the two chalcogen layers are different. The resulting symmetry breaking produces a permanent out-of-plane electric dipole and a chemically asymmetric pair of surfaces \cite{lu_janus_natnano, zhang_janus_acs_nano}. First-principles density-functional theory (DFT) studies have shown that this asymmetry produces face-dependent adsorption behaviour: Janus ZrSSe and MoSSe display systematically stronger physisorption of gas molecules and nucleobases on the Se-terminated surface than on the S-terminated surface \cite{zrsse_dft_gas_sensing, mossse_dna_acsanm_2026}. For lithium specifically, single-Li adsorption on Janus monolayers (MoSSe, MoSTe, WSSe) shows distinct binding energies and migration barriers on the two faces \cite{nguyen_wsse_li_dft, mosy_dft_2025}, with the trend generally favouring the Se face --- although the opposite trend has been reported for Janus carbo-chalcogenides such as Nb$_2$CSSe \cite{nb2csse_anode_2026}. Whether this face selectivity \textit{survives} when the Janus filler is embedded within a polymer electrolyte --- where Li$^+$ is preferentially solvated by PEO ether oxygens rather than being exposed to a vacuum interface --- has, to our knowledge, not been investigated.

\paragraph{This work.} We address this gap by reporting an atomistic molecular dynamics (MD) study of a single Janus \WSSe monolayer immersed in PEO/\LiTFSI at $400$~K (above the PEO melting point and within the practical operating window of SSLBs). The questions we address are: (1) Does the canonical PEO ether-oxygen solvation of Li$^+$ remain intact in the presence of the 2D filler, or is it disrupted? (2) Does Li$^+$ transport develop a measurable anisotropy due to the impermeable WSSe barrier? (3) Does the Janus S/Se asymmetry imprint itself on the local Li$^+$ distribution around the sheet, despite the intervening polymer solvation shell? We extract self-diffusion coefficients resolved into in-plane and out-of-plane components, Li-O radial distribution functions, z-resolved Li$^+$ density profiles relative to the WSSe midplane, and face-resolved Li$^+$ contact statistics with proper block-averaged error bars. The results provide the first quantitative atomistic picture of a Janus-TMD-reinforced composite polymer electrolyte and establish design rules for future directional ion-transport CPEs.

%% ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Simulation Methodology}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{System composition and atom typing}
The simulated system shown in figure ~\ref{fig:PEO_system} contains $7024$ atoms organised in three subsystems: (i) a $40 \times 40 \times 3.37$~\AA$^3$ Janus \WSSe monolayer ($144$ W, $144$ S, $144$ Se atoms; $12 \times 12$ supercell of the in-plane lattice constant $a=3.317$~\AA), built from the relaxed crystal structure with S occupying the top ($+z$) face and Se the bottom ($-z$) face, separated by a $3.15$~\AA{} interlayer gap; (ii) a PEO matrix consisting of $40$ chains each of $20$ monomer units (--CH$_2$--CH$_2$--O--), generated by an unbiased random-walk placement algorithm to produce realistic Gaussian coil statistics; and (iii) $62$ Li$^+$ and $62$ TFSI$^-$ ions giving an ether-oxygen-to-lithium ratio of EO:Li = $16{:}1$, representative of the optimal salt concentration for PEO/\LiTFSI \cite{marchiori_electrochim_2017}. The simulation cell employs periodic boundaries in all three directions with the WSSe sheet lying in the $xy$-plane centred at the box midplane in $z$.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.3\textwidth]{figures/PEO-system.png}
\caption{Equilibrated atomic structure of the PEO--LiTFSI polymer electrolyte
in contact with a central Janus WSSe monolayer, with electrolyte on both faces
of the layer. The Janus monolayer exposes a sulfur-terminated face on one side
and a selenium-terminated face on the other. Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, lithium,
nitrogen, sulfur, fluorine, tungsten, and selenium atoms are shown in gray,
white, red, lilac, dark blue, olive, light blue, blue, and green, respectively.}
\label{fig:PEO_system}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Force fields}
Intra-WSSe interactions were modelled with the modified Stillinger--Weber (SW) potential of Jiang \cite{jiang_sw_tmd_2019}, which has been extensively validated for thermal and mechanical properties of TMDs including the Janus subfamily. The PEO matrix and TFSI$^-$ anion were described using the all-atom OPLS-AA force field, with partial charges and bonded parameters taken from the standard parameter set. Following best practice for high-temperature MD of PEO/\LiTFSI \cite{he_jpcb_2020_polarity}, Li$^+$ and TFSI$^-$ partial charges were scaled by a factor of $0.75$ to compensate for the omission of explicit electronic polarisation, an approximation that has been shown to recover both the experimental Li$^+$ diffusivity and the correct $D_{\mathrm{TFSI}}/D_{\mathrm{Li}^+}$ ratio. Cross interactions between the WSSe atoms and the polymer/electrolyte atoms were generated using Lorentz--Berthelot mixing of UFF Lennard-Jones parameters for W, S, and Se, applied as a \texttt{hybrid/overlay} of the SW and \texttt{lj/cut/coul/long} pair styles. Long-range electrostatics were treated with the particle--particle particle--mesh (PPPM) solver with $10^{-4}$ accuracy. Special-bond exclusions were set to (0, 0, 0.5) for 1--2, 1--3, and 1--4 interactions, consistent with OPLS-AA conventions.

\subsection{Equilibration and production protocol}
All MD was performed in the LAMMPS package (29~Aug~2024 stable release) on the Param Brahma HPC cluster using a Velocity-Verlet integrator with a $1$~fs timestep. Following an initial energy minimisation with the WSSe sheet held fixed, the system was annealed in NVT from $300$ to $600$~K over $2$~ns, then cooled to $400$~K over a further $2$~ns, with a weak \texttt{spring/self} restraint (force constant $10$~kcal/mol/\AA$^2$) applied to the WSSe atoms to suppress sheet drift during the violent thermal annealing. The resulting structure was then placed in a box rescaled to give an initial density of $1.20$~g/cm$^3$ (close to the literature value for PEO/\LiTFSI at this composition and temperature) and equilibrated for $10$~ns in NVT at $400$~K, with a \texttt{fix recenter} pinning the WSSe centre-of-mass at $z = L_z/2$ to maintain symmetric polymer distribution on both faces. The production stage was a $30$~ns NVT run at $400$~K under the identical box, during which the trajectory and analysis outputs described below were collected. Stress--strain response under uniaxial elongation in $z$ is reported separately and the corresponding analysis is in progress.

\subsection{Analysis observables}
Four observables were extracted from production:
(1) \textit{Directional mean-squared displacement (MSD)} of Li$^+$, computed using a sliding time-origin window to maximise statistical sampling, decomposed into in-plane ($x$, $y$) and out-of-plane ($z$) components after removal of Li-group centre-of-mass drift. Self-diffusion coefficients $\Dxy$ and $\Dz$ were obtained from linear fits in the diffusive regime ($3$--$10$~ns lag window).
(2) \textit{Li$^+$--ether-O radial distribution function} $g_{\mathrm{Li-O}}(r)$, averaged over the production run and integrated to extract the first-shell coordination number $n_{\mathrm{coord}}$ at the standard cutoff of $r < 3.5$~\AA.
(3) \textit{Li$^+$ z-density profile} measured relative to the WSSe sheet midplane.
(4) \textit{Face-resolved Li$^+$ contact statistics}: the number of Li$^+$ ions per frame found within cutoff distances of $r_c = 3$, $4$, $5$, $6$, and $8$~\AA{} of the S-face (above the sheet) and Se-face (below). Block averaging into 5 statistically-independent blocks of $\sim6$~ns each was used to obtain unbiased standard errors and significance levels.

%% ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Results and Discussion}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Validation: Li$^+$ solvation by PEO ether oxygens}\label{sec:res_lio}
We begin by verifying that the standard PEO/\LiTFSI Li$^+$ solvation environment is reproduced in our composite system. The Li-O radial distribution function (\fig{fig:results}b) exhibits a sharp first-shell peak at $r = 2.13$~\AA{} with $g(r)_{\mathrm{max}} \approx 35$, and a running coordination number that saturates at $n_{\mathrm{coord}} = 6.1$ within the first solvation shell ($r < 3.5$~\AA). Both the peak position and the coordination number are in close agreement with prior reports for neat PEO/\LiTFSI: Marchiori et al.~\cite{marchiori_electrochim_2017} report a Li-O$_{\mathrm{PEO}}$ first-shell peak at $2.04$~\AA{} and $n_{\mathrm{coord}} \approx 6$, and the cross-linked PEO/\LiTFSI MD of Wettstein et al.~\cite{wettstein_crosslinked_peo_2022} similarly finds a maximum-probability EO coordination of $6$ for the TFSI system. The retention of the canonical solvation shell in the presence of the Janus filler confirms two important points: (i) the cross-interaction parameters chosen via Lorentz--Berthelot mixing of UFF terms do not artificially disrupt the polymer--cation solvation, and (ii) Li$^+$ transport in our composite still proceeds via the standard PEO ether-oxygen hopping mechanism rather than via any pathological binding to the WSSe surface.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{figures/fig2_LiO_coordination.pdf}
\caption{Li$^+$--ether-oxygen radial distribution function $g(r)$ (green) and running coordination number $n(r)$ (orange dashed). The first-shell peak at $2.13$~\AA{} and $n_{\mathrm{coord}}=6.1$ within $3.5$~\AA{} match the canonical PEO/LiTFSI solvation environment.}
\label{fig:rdf}
\end{figure}

