\subsection{Anisotropic Li$^+$ transport}\label{sec:res_msd}
\Fig{fig:results}a shows the time-resolved Li$^+$ mean-squared displacement (MSD) decomposed into in-plane and out-of-plane components. The two components diverge cleanly from the earliest accessible lag time, with in-plane displacement growing several times faster than out-of-plane displacement over the full observation window. Linear fits to the $3$--$10$~ns sliding-window MSD yield $\Dxy = (4.35 \pm 0.01) \times 10^{-7}$~cm$^2$/s and $\Dz = (1.82 \pm 0.02) \times 10^{-7}$~cm$^2$/s, giving an anisotropy ratio $\Dxy/\Dz = 2.4$.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{figures/fig1_msd_anisotropy.pdf}
\caption{Sliding-window Li$^+$ MSD decomposed into in-plane ($xy$, red) and out-of-plane ($z$, blue) components, with shaded $\pm$standard error. Linear fits in the $3$--$10$~ns window give $\Dxy=4.35\times10^{-7}$ and $\Dz=1.82\times10^{-7}$~cm$^2$/s, an anisotropy ratio of $2.4$.}
\label{fig:msd}
\end{figure}

Before interpreting these magnitudes we examine the diffusive character of the motion. \Fig{fig:msd_loglog} presents the same data on logarithmic axes together with a reference line of unit slope corresponding to ideal Einstein diffusion ($\mathrm{MSD} \propto t$). The Li$^+$ MSD remains sub-diffusive throughout the accessible window, with a local logarithmic slope (diffusion exponent) of $\beta_{xy} \approx 0.67$ and $\beta_{z} \approx 0.66$ in the $3$--$10$~ns interval rather than the value of unity expected for the fully developed diffusive regime. This behaviour is a well-documented signature of PEO-based electrolytes: the strong coupling between Li$^+$ motion and slow PEO segmental relaxation produces an extended sub-diffusive plateau, with reported intermediate-time exponents of $\alpha \approx 0.6$ attributed to topological constraints on the polymer chains~\cite{wettstein_crosslinked_peo_2022}. The diffusivities reported here should therefore be understood as \emph{apparent} (effective) diffusivities extracted from the most diffusive part of the accessible trajectory, rather than fully converged Einstein coefficients, which would require substantially longer trajectories (of order $100$~ns or more) to reach.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{figures/fig1b_msd_loglog.pdf}
\caption{Log--log representation of the directional Li$^+$ MSD with a reference line of unit slope (ideal Einstein diffusion). The MSD remains sub-diffusive over the accessible window ($\beta_{xy}\approx0.67$, $\beta_{z}\approx0.66$), characteristic of PEO segmental dynamics. The reported diffusivities are therefore apparent values; the anisotropy ratio is robust as both components share the same convergence factor.}
\label{fig:msd_loglog}
\end{figure}

Two points nonetheless make the transport analysis robust and physically meaningful. First, the apparent magnitude of $\Dxy$ falls squarely within the $1$--$5 \times 10^{-7}$~cm$^2$/s range reported for PEO/\LiTFSI in the same temperature window using comparable charge-scaled force-field protocols~\cite{he_jpcb_2020_polarity}, confirming that the simulation captures the correct transport scale. Second, and more importantly, the anisotropy \emph{ratio} $\Dxy/\Dz$ is largely insensitive to the sub-diffusive limitation, because both directional components are sampled over the identical time window and are affected by the same incomplete-convergence factor, which cancels in the ratio. The anisotropy ratio of $2.4$ is, to our knowledge, the first quantitative atomistic measurement of filler-induced Li$^+$ transport anisotropy in a PEO/\LiTFSI composite. Experimental conductivity measurements on 2D-filler CPEs (e.g., MXene/PEO/\LiTFSI~\cite{pan_nanoscale_adv_2019_mxene}, BP/PEO~\cite{rojaee_2d_bp}) routinely infer anisotropy phenomenologically through aligned-versus-isotropic film comparisons, but direct decomposition of the diffusivity into in-plane and out-of-plane channels --- which atomistic MD provides naturally --- has been absent.

The physical origin of the anisotropy is unambiguous: the WSSe sheet is impermeable to Li$^+$ on the timescales accessed, so every cross-plane trajectory requires Li$^+$ to detour around the periodic sheet edges. Within the supercell this constitutes a topological barrier that suppresses cross-plane displacement while leaving in-plane diffusion essentially unhindered, directly producing the observed $\Dxy/\Dz > 2$.


\subsection{Janus-face-selective Li$^+$ distribution}\label{sec:res_janus}
The clearest signature of the Janus chemistry appears in the spatial distribution of Li$^+$ relative to the WSSe sheet. \Fig{fig:results}c shows the $z$-resolved Li$^+$ density profile, with the origin set at the sheet midplane. The profile is broadly symmetric about the sheet, confirming the success of the \texttt{recenter} protocol in maintaining balanced polymer slabs on both faces. However, on closer inspection, the first Li$^+$ peak adjacent to the Se-face (centred near $z \approx -7$~\AA) is noticeably sharper and located closer to the sheet than the corresponding feature on the S-face side, suggesting a slight Se-face preference at short range.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{figures/fig3_Li_zdensity.pdf}
\caption{Li$^+$ number-density distribution along $z$ relative to the WSSe sheet midplane (yellow band). Vertical dashed lines mark the S- and Se-face positions. The first Li$^+$ peak adjacent to the Se-face is sharper and closer to the sheet than its S-face counterpart.}
\label{fig:zdensity}
\end{figure}

This impression is confirmed quantitatively by face-resolved Li$^+$ contact counting (\fig{fig:results}d, Table~\ref{tab:contacts}). At the strictest cutoff ($r_c = 3$~\AA), the Se-face holds $1.13 \pm 0.08$ Li$^+$ per frame while the S-face holds only $0.32 \pm 0.10$, corresponding to a difference of $0.81 \pm 0.13$ Li$^+$ per frame --- a statistical significance of $6.2\sigma$. The Se-face preference persists at $r_c = 4$~\AA{} ($3.7\sigma$). At intermediate distances ($r_c = 5$--$6$~\AA) the two faces are statistically indistinguishable, while at $r_c = 8$~\AA{} the Se-face again accumulates more cations ($3.4\sigma$). The strong, statistically clean Se-face preference at close contact is consistent with the trend reported by gas-phase DFT for Janus chalcogenides: Janus MoSSe, ZrSSe, and Nb-based monolayers exhibit systematically stronger physisorption of small molecules and atoms on the Se-terminated surface than on the S-terminated surface \cite{zrsse_dft_gas_sensing, mossse_dna_acsanm_2026, mosy_dft_2025}. The face selectivity has been attributed to the larger atomic radius, higher polarisability, and softer electronic environment of Se relative to S, which together favour close approach of polarisable adsorbates including cations~\cite{nguyen_wsse_li_dft}.

The novel point of the present work is that this Se-face preference \textit{survives} within a solvating polymer matrix --- a regime that the prior gas-phase DFT literature could not access. Even though every Li$^+$ in our system is coordinated by approximately six PEO ether oxygens (\sect{sec:res_lio}), the small fraction of Li$^+$ that visits the WSSe interface partitions strongly toward the Se-face. The physical picture is that PEO solvation does not screen out the Janus dipole entirely; instead, the chemical contrast between S and Se still biases Li$^+$ partitioning, albeit acting on the small surface-visiting population rather than on the bulk-solvated majority.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Face-resolved Li$^+$ contact statistics from $30$~ns production. Errors are block-averaged standard errors over $5$ blocks. $\sigma$ denotes the statistical significance of the S-vs-Se difference.}
\label{tab:contacts}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\hline
Cutoff $r_c$ (\AA) & S-face (per frame) & Se-face (per frame) & Difference & $\sigma$ \\
\hline
3 & $0.32 \pm 0.10$ & $1.13 \pm 0.08$ & $-0.81 \pm 0.13$ & $6.2$ \\
4 & $1.02 \pm 0.17$ & $1.74 \pm 0.09$ & $-0.72 \pm 0.19$ & $3.7$ \\
