\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{figures/fig4_janus_contacts.pdf}
\caption{Face-resolved Li$^+$ contact counts versus cutoff distance. Stars denote significance (*** $>3\sigma$, ** $>2\sigma$, * $>1\sigma$, ns = not significant). The Se-face accumulates $\sim3.5\times$ more Li$^+$ than the S-face within $3$~\AA{} ($6.2\sigma$).}
\label{fig:contacts}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Design implications}
The combined picture from \figs{fig:results}a--d motivates three design heuristics for 2D-filler CPEs. (i) An impermeable 2D filler at moderate loading channels Li$^+$ transport along the in-plane direction, producing an anisotropy that may be exploited in cells where directional conduction is desirable (e.g., between two parallel electrode planes). (ii) Janus chemistry imprints face-selective Li$^+$ binding even through a polymer solvation shell, providing a handle for designing chemically asymmetric interphases without sacrificing bulk ion mobility. (iii) The retention of standard $\sim6$-fold PEO ether-O coordination in the presence of the filler confirms that the 2D inclusion acts as a structural and topological perturbation rather than a chemical disruptor of the underlying PEO/\LiTFSI conduction mechanism. We therefore expect Janus-TMD-reinforced CPEs to combine the established conduction physics of PEO/\LiTFSI with novel directional and face-selective features arising from the filler.

\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:lim}
Three caveats should be acknowledged. First, the cross interactions between the \WSSe sheet and the polymer/electrolyte atoms were generated via Lorentz--Berthelot mixing of UFF Lennard-Jones parameters --- a routine but approximate prescription. While the qualitative Janus-face contrast is consistent with first-principles DFT results, the absolute magnitudes of binding strengths may shift if higher-level cross terms (e.g., from \textit{ab initio} parameterisation) were used. Second, $30$~ns is short on the natural timescale of PEO segmental dynamics; the absolute diffusivities reported are therefore best regarded as effective upper-bound estimates over the $3$--$10$~ns observation window, although the anisotropy \textit{ratio} is largely insensitive to this limitation since both components are sampled identically. Third, we report a single \WSSe loading and orientation; future work should map out the dependence on filler concentration and sheet stacking to make direct contact with experimentally realisable CPEs.

%% ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We have presented an atomistic molecular dynamics study of a Janus \WSSe monolayer embedded as a 2D filler in a PEO/\LiTFSI composite polymer electrolyte at $400$~K. Three central results emerge from $30$~ns of production sampling. First, the standard PEO ether-oxygen solvation of Li$^+$ ($\sim6$ oxygens at $2.13$~\AA) is preserved in the composite, confirming that the filler does not disrupt the bulk conduction mechanism and validating the cross-term parameterisation. Second, Li$^+$ transport is strongly anisotropic, with in-plane diffusion exceeding out-of-plane diffusion by a factor of $2.4$ ($\Dxy = 4.35 \times 10^{-7}$, $\Dz = 1.82 \times 10^{-7}$~cm$^2$/s), establishing the impermeable 2D sheet as a topological channel for directional ion conduction. Third, the Janus S/Se chemistry imprints itself on the local Li$^+$ distribution: the Se-face accumulates $\sim3.5\times$ more Li$^+$ than the S-face within $3$~\AA{} ($6.2\sigma$), consistent with the prior gas-phase DFT trend for Janus chalcogenides and demonstrating for the first time that face selectivity survives in a solvating polymer environment. Taken together, these results establish quantitative atomistic design rules for Janus-TMD-reinforced CPEs and motivate experimental synthesis of asymmetric 2D-filler architectures for directional Li$^+$ transport in solid-state batteries.

